  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <!--
        Pinned up, not down. Calcite reaches commons-logging twice: 1.1.3 through
        aggdesigner-algorithm and 1.2 through commons-dbcp2. Maven's nearest-wins picked the
        older of the two, so the classpath carried 1.1.3 while something in the tree was built
        against 1.2, which is which is the shape of failure that appears as a NoSuchMethodError under a
        code path nobody exercised before production.

        Found by requireUpperBoundDeps, which is why that rule is on.
      -->
      <!--
        Guava and its annotation companion are reached at several versions across Calcite, Hadoop
        and Arrow, and nearest-wins was picking an older one than parts of the tree were built
        against. Pinned to the highest any path asks for, which is what requireUpperBoundDeps is
        checking: a caller compiled against a newer API must not find an older class.

        33.5.0-android appears in the tree and is deliberately not the pin. The android flavour
        drops the Java 8+ types the jre flavour has, so choosing it by version number alone would
        satisfy the rule and break the classpath.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.google.guava</groupId>
        <artifactId>guava</artifactId>
        <version>33.7.1-jre</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.google.errorprone</groupId>
        <artifactId>error_prone_annotations</artifactId>
        <version>2.50.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!--
        Jackson arrives through Arrow, Spring Boot and Hadoop, which disagree; commons-codec
        through Hadoop and the HTTP clients. Each was resolving to the lowest version any path
        asked for, so the classpath carried an older Jackson than parts of the tree were compiled
        against. Pinned to the highest demanded, together, because the Jackson artifacts must move
        as a set: databind against a different core is its own failure.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-core</artifactId>
        <version>2.22.1</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        <version>2.22.1</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-annotations</artifactId>
        <version>2.22</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>commons-codec</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-codec</artifactId>
        <version>1.21.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!--
        Protobuf was the fourth library in the list above that arrived only transitively: gRPC
        brings 4.33.4 into pravaha-flight, Calcite's Avatica 3.25.5 wherever it is on the path, and
        nearest-wins decided which a module got. The Kafka source's `format: protobuf` reads a
        descriptor set with DynamicMessage and so depends on it by name, which makes the version
        somebody's decision rather than the tree's.

        Pinned UP to 4.33.4, the highest anything here asks for, which is what requireUpperBoundDeps
        demands and what pravaha-flight already resolved: the 4.x runtime reads code generated by
        3.25 and later, so Avatica keeps working against it, and pinning down to 3.25.8 instead
        would fail the rule the moment gRPC asks for 4.33.4.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.google.protobuf</groupId>
        <artifactId>protobuf-java</artifactId>
        <version>4.33.4</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.xerial.snappy</groupId>
        <artifactId>snappy-java</artifactId>
        <version>1.1.10.4</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-datatype-jsr310</artifactId>
        <version>2.22.1</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.yaml</groupId>
        <artifactId>snakeyaml</artifactId>
        <version>2.6</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>commons-logging</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-logging</artifactId>
        <version>1.2</version>
      </dependency>

      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${junit.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.agrona</groupId>
        <artifactId>agrona</artifactId>
        <version>${agrona.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.jctools</groupId>
        <artifactId>jctools-core</artifactId>
        <version>${jctools.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
        <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
        <version>${slf4j.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
        <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
        <version>${assertj.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>net.jqwik</groupId>
        <artifactId>jqwik</artifactId>
        <version>${jqwik.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
        <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
        <version>${archunit.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
        <artifactId>testcontainers-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${testcontainers.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.aerospike</groupId>
        <artifactId>aerospike-client-jdk21</artifactId>
        <version>${aerospike.client.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <!--
        Apache donated the DataStax driver to the Cassandra project; the artifact moved from
        com.datastax.oss to org.apache.cassandra at 4.18. Only java-driver-core is pulled in;
        the query-builder module is not, because this plugin builds its one CQL shape (a
        token-range predicate) as a literal string rather than depending on a builder for it.
      -->
      <!--
        The SHADED core, not java-driver-core: the driver relocates its own Netty and Jackson under
        com.datastax.oss.driver.shaded. Arrow Flight's gRPC needs Netty 4.2 and the plain driver pulls
        Netty 4.1, and every connector now ships inside the one server jar, where a classpath holds
        exactly one version of each library: Maven would have kept one, and the other side would have
        failed at runtime rather than at build time.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.cassandra</groupId>
        <artifactId>java-driver-core-shaded</artifactId>
        <version>${cassandra.driver.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- The Kafka sink's client. Its compression codecs are excluded where it is used; see that pom. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.kafka</groupId>
        <artifactId>kafka-clients</artifactId>
        <version>${kafka.clients.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <!--
        Pinned for TEST scope only, and only because excluding jnr-posix from the driver changed
        which version wins. Testcontainers asks for jnr-ffi 2.1.7, the driver asked for 2.2.11, and
        with the driver's request gone the older one is selected. It is not a runtime dependency of
        anything here; pinning keeps the offline build resolvable and stops a test-scope transitive
        drifting backwards on its own.
      -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.jnr</groupId>
        <artifactId>jnr-ffi</artifactId>
        <version>2.2.11</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.jnr</groupId>
        <artifactId>jnr-posix</artifactId>
        <version>3.1.15</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.jnr</groupId>
        <artifactId>jffi</artifactId>
        <version>1.3.9</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.arrow</groupId>
        <artifactId>arrow-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${arrow.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjdk.jmh</groupId>
        <artifactId>jmh-core</artifactId>
        <version>${jmh.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.openjdk.jmh</groupId>
        <artifactId>jmh-generator-annprocess</artifactId>
        <version>${jmh.version}</version>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- Every module gets the test stack. Nothing else is inherited. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.assertj</groupId>
      <artifactId>assertj-core</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <pluginManagement>
      <plugins>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.compiler.plugin.version}</version>
          <configuration>
            <release>${maven.compiler.release}</release>
            <compilerArgs>
              <arg>-Xlint:all,-serial,-processing</arg>
              <arg>-parameters</arg>
            </compilerArgs>
            <showDeprecation>true</showDeprecation>
          </configuration>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.surefire.plugin.version}</version>
          <configuration>
            <argLine>@{jacoco.surefire.argLine} -Xss4m</argLine>
            <excludes>
              <exclude>**/*IT.java</exclude>
            </excludes>
            <systemPropertyVariables>
              <!-- Testcontainers' docker-java client asks for API 1.32 unless told otherwise, and
                   Docker Engine 29 refuses anything below 1.40, which Testcontainers reports as "no
                   Docker", so every container test skipped silently on a machine that had one. -->
              <api.version>${docker.api.version}</api.version>
            </systemPropertyVariables>
          </configuration>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.failsafe.plugin.version}</version>
          <configuration>
            <systemPropertyVariables>
              <api.version>${docker.api.version}</api.version>
            </systemPropertyVariables>
          </configuration>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.jar.plugin.version}</version>
          <configuration>
            <archive>
              <manifest>
                <!-- Implementation-Title and Implementation-Version, from the pom. Without them
                     Package.getImplementationVersion() returned null and the node reported a
                     hard-coded version in /api/v1/status and /actuator/info: 0.1.0-SNAPSHOT on a
                     0.1.1 build, which is the first thing a tester would read and get wrong. -->
                <addDefaultImplementationEntries>true</addDefaultImplementationEntries>
              </manifest>
            </archive>
          </configuration>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.dependency.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>

        <!-- Pinned explicitly: the enforcer's requirePluginVersions rule rejects
             lifecycle-bound plugins that fall back to Maven's own defaults, because
             those defaults drift between Maven versions and break reproducible builds. -->
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-clean-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.clean.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-resources-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.resources.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-install-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.install.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-deploy-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.deploy.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-site-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.site.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.javadoc.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-toolchains-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${maven.toolchains.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>

        <plugin>
          <groupId>com.github.siom79.japicmp</groupId>
          <artifactId>japicmp-maven-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>${japicmp.plugin.version}</version>
        </plugin>
      </plugins>
    </pluginManagement>

    <plugins>
      <!-- ==== P0-02: enforce the invariants the design depends on ==== -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${maven.enforcer.plugin.version}</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>enforce-build-environment</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>enforce</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <!--
                  Four libraries (gRPC, protobuf, Jackson and Guava) arrive only transitively,
                  through three BOMs that do not agree: Arrow's, Spring Boot's and Hadoop's. Their
                  versions were therefore decided by Maven's nearest-wins rule, which is to say by
                  the shape of the dependency tree rather than by anybody. That is how the Netty
                  conflict happened, and Netty was pinned by hand after it bit.

                  requireUpperBoundDeps rather than dependencyConvergence, deliberately. Convergence
                  fails when an artifact is reached by two paths at all, including two paths to the
                  SAME version differing only in scope, of which this build has three and none is a
                  conflict. A rule that cries wolf earns a fail=false and is then ignored.

                  This one fails only when something asks for a NEWER version than the one resolved,
                  which is the case that actually breaks at runtime: a library compiled against an
                  API the version on the classpath does not have.
                -->
                <requireUpperBoundDeps />
                <requireMavenVersion>
                  <version>[3.9,)</version>
                </requireMavenVersion>
                <requireJavaVersion>
                  <version>[21,)</version>
                </requireJavaVersion>
                <banDuplicatePomDependencyVersions />
                <requirePluginVersions>
                  <banSnapshots>true</banSnapshots>
                </requirePluginVersions>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ==== P0-02: one format, applied not argued about ==== -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>com.diffplug.spotless</groupId>
        <artifactId>spotless-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${spotless.plugin.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <java>
            <includes>
              <include>src/main/java/**/*.java</include>
              <include>src/test/java/**/*.java</include>
            </includes>
            <palantirJavaFormat>
              <version>${palantir.format.version}</version>
            </palantirJavaFormat>
            <removeUnusedImports />
            <importOrder>
              <file>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/config/spotless/pravaha.importorder</file>
            </importOrder>
            <licenseHeader>
              <file>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/config/spotless/license-header.txt</file>
            </licenseHeader>
            <trimTrailingWhitespace />
            <endWithNewline />
          </java>
          <pom>
            <sortPom>
              <expandEmptyElements>false</expandEmptyElements>
              <spaceBeforeCloseEmptyElement>true</spaceBeforeCloseEmptyElement>
              <nrOfIndentSpace>2</nrOfIndentSpace>
              <sortDependencies>none</sortDependencies>
              <sortPlugins>none</sortPlugins>
              <sortModules>false</sortModules>
              <sortProperties>false</sortProperties>
            </sortPom>
          </pom>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>spotless-check</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
            <phase>validate</phase>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>

      <!-- ==== P0-02: coverage gate ==== -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${jacoco.plugin.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <skip>${jacoco.skip}</skip>
        </configuration>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-prepare</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
            <configuration>
              <propertyName>jacoco.surefire.argLine</propertyName>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-report</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
            <phase>verify</phase>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>jacoco-check</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>check</goal>
            </goals>
            <phase>verify</phase>
            <configuration>
              <rules>
                <rule>
                  <element>BUNDLE</element>
                  <limits>
                    <limit>
                      <counter>LINE</counter>
                      <value>COVEREDRATIO</value>
                      <minimum>${jacoco.line.coverage}</minimum>
                    </limit>
                  </limits>
                </rule>
              </rules>
            </configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <!-- Integration tests (Testcontainers). Off by default: the fast loop must stay fast. -->
    <profile>
      <id>it</id>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <goals>
                  <goal>integration-test</goal>
                  <goal>verify</goal>
                </goals>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>

    <!-- The Python SDK and the console. Behind a profile so a Python toolchain is not required to
         build the engine; the Java SDK is always built, because its jar is a deliverable.

         The executions live in pravaha-it rather than here. A parent's phases run before its
         children in the reactor, so binding them to this pom ran the Python tests against whatever
         Java classes were left over from a previous build — which hid a renumbered error code for
         a whole build and would hide anything else the same way. -->
    <profile>
      <id>python</id>
      <properties>
        <pravaha.python.tests.skip>false</pravaha.python.tests.skip>
        <!--
          The virtualenvs the two READMEs tell you to create. Set here rather than by profile
          activation, because activation does not interpolate this property and a profile that
          silently never fires is worse than one that is explicit.

          Requires `make install` in sdk/python and in console first. Override with
          -Dpython.executable=... to use an interpreter of your own.
        -->
        <python.executable>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/sdk/python/.venv/bin/python</python.executable>
        <console.executable>${maven.multiModuleProjectDirectory}/console/.venv/bin/python</console.executable>
      </properties>
    </profile>

    <!-- JMH benchmarks. -->
    <profile>
      <id>bench</id>
      <properties>
        <benchmarks.skip>false</benchmarks.skip>
      </properties>
    </profile>

    <!-- Error Prone + NullAway. Opt-in until P0-02b lands it in the default build. -->
    <profile>
      <id>ep</id>
      <properties>
        <errorprone.skip>false</errorprone.skip>
      </properties>
    </profile>

    <!-- Design 4.6: the FFM MemoryAccess implementation, JDK 22+ only. -->
    <profile>
      <id>ffm</id>
      <activation>
        <jdk>[22,)</jdk>
      </activation>
    </profile>

    <!-- Everything, as CI runs it on main. -->
    <profile>
      <id>all</id>
      <properties>
        <benchmarks.skip>false</benchmarks.skip>
        <errorprone.skip>false</errorprone.skip>
      </properties>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
